1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,429
In the year 499 before the common era, the Greek cities of the Ionian coast — today the western edge of Turkey — rose against the Persian kings who owned them .

2
00:00:12,779 --> 00:00:18,350
They burned Sardis, the great inland capital , and the fire was never forgiven.

3
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:25,986
Darius, king of kings, swore to remember Athens, because Athens had lent the rebels a few ships .

4
00:00:26,336 --> 00:00:29,336
Ten years later he collected the debt.

5
00:00:29,686 --> 00:00:34,829
A Persian fleet landed at the bay of Marathon, north of Athens .

6
00:00:35,179 --> 00:00:45,036
The Athenians, a few thousand citizen-soldiers, walked out to meet it with a small band of Plataeans — the only city that came .

7
00:00:45,386 --> 00:00:52,243
Herodotus says they attacked at a run, the first Greeks ever to charge at a sprint .

8
00:00:52,593 --> 00:01:00,307
What followed was shocking: some six thousand four hundred Persians dead against one hundred and ninety-two Athenians .

9
00:01:00,657 --> 00:01:07,943
The famous runner of Marathon — the man who sprints to Athens and dies — is not in Herodotus.

10
00:01:08,293 --> 00:01:17,293
The runner Herodotus does give us is Pheidippides, who ran from Athens to Sparta in two days to ask for help .

11
00:01:17,643 --> 00:01:22,786
Where the facts are better than the legend, we keep the facts.

12
00:01:23,136 --> 00:01:40,707
Herodotus gives us one perfect image of the cause: Darius, hearing that Sardis had burned, asked who the Athenians were, and then demanded an archer's bow, and kept a servant to whisper to him at every meal, Master, remember the Athenians .

13
00:01:41,057 --> 00:01:45,343
The empire had a long memory, and a short temper.

14
00:01:45,693 --> 00:01:55,979
Darius died with the debt unpaid; his son Xerxes came himself, in 480, with an army the sources insist was too large to count .

15
00:01:56,329 --> 00:02:08,757
At the hot springs of Thermopylae a few thousand Greeks held the narrow road for three days, until a local man showed the Persians a path around the mountain .

16
00:02:09,107 --> 00:02:15,536
Leonidas and his three hundred Spartans stayed, by law and by choice, and died there .

17
00:02:15,886 --> 00:02:20,171
But the real weapon of that year was a strait.

18
00:02:20,521 --> 00:02:34,664
Themistocles, the Athenian who had convinced his city to build a navy with silver from a new mine , lured the enormous Persian fleet into the narrow water at Salamis, where numbers meant nothing .

19
00:02:35,014 --> 00:02:41,443
In the crush, the big fleet beat itself against the rocks and its own oars .

20
00:02:41,793 --> 00:02:51,221
A man who was there, the playwright Aeschylus, put it in a line: the sea was full of wrecks and drowning men .

21
00:02:51,571 --> 00:03:02,714
Before all this, Athens had asked the oracle at Delphi what to do, and the oracle answered in riddles about wooden walls and a divine Salamis .

22
00:03:03,064 --> 00:03:10,779
It was Themistocles who read the riddle as ships — and the city staked its life on his reading .

23
00:03:11,129 --> 00:03:19,271
While the pass held at Thermopylae, the same navy had been bleeding the Persians at Artemisium in the north .

24
00:03:19,621 --> 00:03:21,764
One plan, two narrow places.

25
00:03:22,114 --> 00:03:27,257
The next year, 479, the Persian general Mardonius tried Greece by land.

26
00:03:27,607 --> 00:03:34,464
At Plataea the Spartan regent Pausanias commanded the largest Greek army that had ever stood together .

27
00:03:34,814 --> 00:03:44,243
When the lines finally locked, the Persians fought bravely, but armour and long spears won: Mardonius fell and his camp was stormed .

28
00:03:44,593 --> 00:03:56,164
On the same day, says the tradition, the Greek fleet won at Mycale on the Ionian coast — a coincidence so neat the ancients themselves marvelled at it.

29
00:03:56,514 --> 00:03:58,229
The invasion was over.

30
00:03:58,579 --> 00:04:00,721
The small cities had held.

31
00:04:01,071 --> 00:04:02,357
Victory organised itself.

32
00:04:02,707 --> 00:04:11,279
In 478 the island cities and Athens founded a league against Persia, its treasury on the sacred island of Delos .

33
00:04:11,629 --> 00:04:22,771
Year by year the league became an empire, and the treasury moved to Athens — around 454, though the exact date is read from stone and argued .

34
00:04:23,121 --> 00:04:25,693
With the money came the buildings.

35
00:04:26,043 --> 00:04:34,186
Pericles, the cold- voiced aristocrat the people kept re-electing, raised the Parthenon on the ruins of the war .

36
00:04:34,536 --> 00:04:40,536
In the funeral speech Thucydides gives him, Pericles calls Athens the school of Hellas .

37
00:04:40,886 --> 00:04:46,029
It is the best single line the fifth century wrote about itself.

38
00:04:46,379 --> 00:04:53,236
It is also the speech of a man asking the living to pay for the dead.

39
00:04:53,586 --> 00:04:54,871
We hold both.

40
00:04:55,221 --> 00:05:06,364
Under the same men, Athens walled itself to the sea with the Long Walls, so that the city could never again be starved off the land .

41
00:05:06,714 --> 00:05:14,857
Empire, marble, walls, and a democracy that still excluded women, slaves, and most of the people it ruled over.

42
00:05:15,207 --> 00:05:20,350
The school of Hellas taught every one of these lessons at once.

43
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:34,843
Even its safety valve was dramatic: once a year the citizens could scratch a name on a potsherd and banish a man for ten years — ostracism, the demos keeping its tallest heads level .

44
00:05:35,193 --> 00:05:39,907
Thousands of the shards survive; the practice is archaeology, not gossip.

45
00:05:40,257 --> 00:05:43,257
Sparta watched the school become an empire.

46
00:05:43,607 --> 00:05:53,893
The truest cause of the war that followed, says Thucydides, was fear: the growth of Athenian power and the alarm it caused in Sparta .

47
00:05:54,243 --> 00:05:58,957
From 431 the Greek world ate itself for twenty-seven years.

48
00:05:59,307 --> 00:06:13,879
Midway, at the small island of Melos, Athenian envoys argued that the strong do what they can and the weak suffer what they must ; the Melians who refused to submit were killed or enslaved .

49
00:06:14,229 --> 00:06:18,943
Thucydides places the dialogue in his book with almost no comment.

50
00:06:19,293 --> 00:06:21,436
The comment is the placement.

51
00:06:21,786 --> 00:06:29,929
A plague entered the crowded city in the second year and took perhaps a third of it, including Pericles .

52
00:06:30,279 --> 00:06:43,136
Then the empire gambled: a great fleet sent to conquer Sicily, and destroyed at Syracuse in 413 , with the cleverest man in Athens, Alcibiades, having fled to the enemy side .

53
00:06:43,486 --> 00:06:52,914
The end came at Aegospotami in 405, when the Spartan admiral Lysander caught the Athenian fleet beached and cut it to pieces .

54
00:06:53,264 --> 00:07:00,979
In 404 the walls came down to the sound of flute-girls, and the empire was a memory .

55
00:07:01,329 --> 00:07:06,471
The cities kept fighting over the pieces until the north settled it.

56
00:07:06,821 --> 00:07:17,964
Philip of Macedon, a hostage who had studied Greece as a student of its wars, built the army that ended Greek freedom at Chaeronea in 338 .

57
00:07:18,314 --> 00:07:23,029
His son, twenty when Philip was murdered, took that army east.

58
00:07:23,379 --> 00:07:31,093
In four years Alexander crossed the Granicus , broke Darius at Issus , and unmade the Persian empire at Gaugamela .

59
00:07:31,443 --> 00:07:43,443
He burned Persepolis — policy or drunken dare, the sources disagree — and died in Babylon in 323, at thirty-two, with no heir and no will the sources trust .

60
00:07:43,793 --> 00:07:53,221
His generals carved the corpse into kingdoms, and the Greek language became the air of the eastern Mediterranean for a thousand years.

61
00:07:53,571 --> 00:08:08,143
He founded Alexandria on the way, a city that would outlive every kingdom of his successors ; in that city his general Ptolemy's house would gather the library the ancient world is always imagined reading.

62
00:08:08,493 --> 00:08:15,779
That is the strange ledger: the cities that could not share power gave the world its grammar.
